(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-04-diagnosis-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 4: Diagnosis Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A study was made of symptoms and physical findings in 247 patients evaluated for sinusitis. The final diagnosis was made according to x-ray findings (gold standard). Ninety-five patients had sinusitis, and 49 of them also had facial pain. One hundred fifty-two did not have sinusitis, and 79 of these patients had facial pain.\n\nWhat is the sensitivity of facial pain for sinusitis in this study?","choices":["38%","48%","52%","61%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sensitivity is the probability that a person with disease has a positive test: true positives divided by (true positives + false negatives). Here 49 of the 95 patients with sinusitis had facial pain, so 49/95 = 52%. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 9; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 12; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 16; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.1"},{"q":"A study was made of symptoms and physical findings in 247 patients evaluated for sinusitis. The final diagnosis was made according to x-ray findings (gold standard). Ninety-five patients had sinusitis, and 49 of them also had facial pain. One hundred fifty-two did not have sinusitis, and 79 of these patients had facial pain.\n\nWhat is the specificity?","choices":["38%","48%","52%","61%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Specificity is the probability that a person without disease has a negative test: true negatives divided by (true negatives + false positives). Of the 152 patients without sinusitis, 79 had facial pain (false positives), leaving 152 - 79 = 73 true negatives, and 73/152 = 48%. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 9; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 13; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 16; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.2"},{"q":"A study was made of symptoms and physical findings in 247 patients evaluated for sinusitis. The final diagnosis was made according to x-ray findings (gold standard). Ninety-five patients had sinusitis, and 49 of them also had facial pain. One hundred fifty-two did not have sinusitis, and 79 of these patients had facial pain.\n\nIf the doctor thought the patient had sinusitis because the patient had facial pain, for what percent of patients would she be correct?","choices":["38%","48%","52%","61%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This is the positive predictive value: of everyone with a positive test (facial pain), the share who truly have disease. Positives = 49 + 79 = 128, and 49/128 = 38%. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 10; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 24; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 25; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.3"},{"q":"A study was made of symptoms and physical findings in 247 patients evaluated for sinusitis. The final diagnosis was made according to x-ray findings (gold standard). Ninety-five patients had sinusitis, and 49 of them also had facial pain. One hundred fifty-two did not have sinusitis, and 79 of these patients had facial pain.\n\nIf the doctor thought the patient did not have sinusitis because the patient did not have facial pain, for what percent of patients would she be correct?","choices":["38%","48%","52%","61%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is the negative predictive value: of everyone with a negative test (no facial pain), the share who truly are disease free. Negatives = 46 + 73 = 119, and 73/119 = 61%. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 10; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 24; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 25; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.4"},{"q":"A study was made of symptoms and physical findings in 247 patients evaluated for sinusitis. The final diagnosis was made according to x-ray findings (gold standard). Ninety-five patients had sinusitis, and 49 of them also had facial pain. One hundred fifty-two did not have sinusitis, and 79 of these patients had facial pain.\n\nHow common was sinusitis in this study?","choices":["38%","48%","52%","61%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Prevalence is the proportion of the studied population that has the disease: 95 patients with sinusitis out of 247 total = 38%. It is not affected by the test result at all. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.5"},{"q":"A study was made of symptoms and physical findings in 247 patients evaluated for sinusitis. The final diagnosis was made according to x-ray findings (gold standard). Ninety-five patients had sinusitis, and 49 of them also had facial pain. One hundred fifty-two did not have sinusitis, and 79 of these patients had facial pain.\n\nWhat is the posttest probability of sinusitis in patients with facial pain in this study?","choices":["38%","48%","52%","61%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Facial pain has a positive likelihood ratio of about 1.0 (sensitivity 0.52 divided by 1 minus specificity, which is 0.52 / 0.52), so it does not change the probability: pretest odds 0.61 x 1.0 = posttest odds 0.61, and 0.61/(1 + 0.61) = 38%, the same as the prevalence and the positive predictive value. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 33; Assigned reading: Likelihood Ratios; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.6"},{"q":"A study was made of symptoms and physical findings in 247 patients evaluated for sinusitis. The final diagnosis was made according to x-ray findings (gold standard). Ninety-five patients had sinusitis, and 49 of them also had facial pain. One hundred fifty-two did not have sinusitis, and 79 of these patients had facial pain.\n\nWhat is the probability of sinusitis in patients assigned a “high probability” by clinicians?","choices":["∼10%","∼20%","∼45%","∼75%","∼90%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pretest odds = 0.38/0.62 = 0.61; multiplying by the likelihood ratio of 4.7 gives posttest odds of about 2.9, and 2.9/(1 + 2.9) = 74%, about 75%. The nomogram gives the same answer. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 33; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 34; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 35; Assigned reading: Likelihood Ratios; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.7"},{"q":"A study was made of symptoms and physical findings in 247 patients evaluated for sinusitis. The final diagnosis was made according to x-ray findings (gold standard). Ninety-five patients had sinusitis, and 49 of them also had facial pain. One hundred fifty-two did not have sinusitis, and 79 of these patients had facial pain.\n\nWhat is the probability of sinusitis in patients assigned an “intermediate probability” by clinicians?","choices":["∼10%","∼20%","∼45%","∼75%","∼90%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An intermediate impression has a likelihood ratio of 1.4, which is indeterminate (0.5 to 2). Pretest odds 0.61 x 1.4 = 0.86, and 0.86/(1 + 0.86) = 46%, about 45%: little change from the 38% starting point. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 33; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 35; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 44; Assigned reading: Likelihood Ratios; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.8"},{"q":"A study was made of symptoms and physical findings in 247 patients evaluated for sinusitis. The final diagnosis was made according to x-ray findings (gold standard). Ninety-five patients had sinusitis, and 49 of them also had facial pain. One hundred fifty-two did not have sinusitis, and 79 of these patients had facial pain.\n\nWhat is the probability of sinusitis in patients assigned a “low probability” by clinicians?","choices":["∼10%","∼20%","∼45%","∼75%","∼90%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A low-probability impression has a likelihood ratio of 0.4 (weak evidence to rule out, 0.2 to 0.5). Pretest odds 0.61 x 0.4 = 0.24, and 0.24/(1 + 0.24) = 20%. The ratio is below 1, so the probability falls from 38%. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 31; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 33; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 44; Assigned reading: Likelihood Ratios; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.9"},{"q":"Which of the following statements is most correct?","choices":["Using diagnostic tests in parallel increases specificity and lowers positive predictive value.","Using diagnostic tests in series increases sensitivity and lowers positive predictive value.","Using diagnostic tests in parallel increases sensitivity and positive predictive value.","Using diagnostic tests in series increases specificity and positive predictive value."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Serial testing requires a positive result on the earlier test before the next is done, which maximizes specificity and positive predictive value (fewer false positives), at the cost of lower sensitivity and negative predictive value. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 46; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 47; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 48; Assigned reading: Likelihood Ratios; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.11"}]};
